<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line" lang="en">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: 
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Justin Deschenaux
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Correspondence to <span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">justin.deschenaux@epfl.ch</span> and <span id="id1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ssahoo@cs.cornell.edu</span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>EPFL, Lausanne, Switzerland
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Caglar Gulcehre
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>EPFL, Lausanne, Switzerland
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Microsoft AI
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Subham Sekhar Sahoo<span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotemark"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotemark: </span>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
          
          
          
          
        </span></span></span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Cornell Tech, NY
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Uniform-state discrete diffusion models excel at few-step generation and guidance due to their ability to self-correct, making them preferred over autoregressive or Masked diffusion models in these settings. However, their sampling quality plateaus with ancestral samplers as the number of steps increases. We introduce a family of Predictor-Corrector (PC) samplers for discrete diffusion that generalize prior methods and apply to arbitrary noise processes. When paired with uniform-state diffusion, our samplers outperform ancestral sampling on both language and image modeling, achieving lower generative perplexity at matched unigram entropy on OpenWebText and better FID/IS scores on CIFAR10. Crucially, unlike conventional samplers, our PC methods continue to improve with more sampling steps.
<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Taken together, these findings call into question the assumption that Masked diffusion is the inevitable future of diffusion-based language modeling.</span>
Beyond sampling, we develop a memory-efficient curriculum for the Gaussian relaxation training phase, reducing training time by 25% and memory by 33% compared to Duo while maintaining comparable perplexity on OpenWebText and LM1B and strong downstream performance. We release code, checkpoints, and a video-tutorial on:</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text">
<a href="https://s-sahoo.github.io/duo-ch2/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://s-sahoo.com/duo-ch2</a></span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S0.F1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21185v1/ancestral_vs_psi_all_nucleus09.svg" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/411;" width="548" height="411"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S0.F1.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21185v1/all_best.svg" id="S0.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/411;" width="548" height="411"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance on Language Modeling and Image Modeling.</span> <math id="S0.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers generalize ReMDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to arbitrary noise distributions. <span id="S0.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Left):</span> Generative perplexity (Gen. PPL; <math id="S0.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) as a function of NFEs, with nucleus sampling <math id="S0.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S0.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers consistently improve with more steps, unlike ancestral sampling which plateaus. Curves are annotated with the average unigram entropy per sequence as a proxy for diversity. <span id="S0.F1.7" class="ltx_text"><span id="S0.F1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Right):</span> On CIFAR-10,</span> <math id="S0.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers achieve better FID (<math id="S0.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) than MDLM (with ReMDM).
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models are powerful generative algorithms that have achieved remarkable success in modeling continuous data domains, including images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, audio <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and videos <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Esser et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blattmann et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Polyak et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Recent advances have extended diffusion models to categorical data, demonstrating their potential for language modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>, molecules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and audio <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ku et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Unlike autoregressive models that generate tokens sequentially from left to right, diffusion language models can decode tokens in parallel and in any order while leveraging bidirectional contextual information. This capability enables the design of language models that can be significantly faster than their autoregressive counterparts while maintaining strong downstream performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labs et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Discrete diffusion models primarily employ one of two noise distributions: a uniform prior or a masked prior that concentrates all probability mass on a special [<span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">mask</span>] token. Unlike <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Masked Diffusion Models</em> (MDMs), which update each token exactly once, <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Uniform-State Diffusion Models</em> (USDMs) allow tokens to be revised multiple times during generation, enabling self-correction. This makes USDMs particularly effective for few-step <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> and guided generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schiff et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, the generation quality of USDMs has not yet matched that of MDMs in high-sampling-step regimes, and USDMs’ modeling capacity, as measured by likelihood, remains inferior to MDMs’. Although <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025a)</a></cite> proposed a curriculum learning strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al., 2009</a>)</cite> that narrows the likelihood gap, this curriculum is computationally expensive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address MDMs’ inability to remask tokens, ReMDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> introduced “Predictor-Corrector” (PC) samplers that generalize and outperform earlier PC methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. These samplers substantially improve the inference time scaling behavior of MDMs. However, PC methods for uniform-state diffusion remain underexplored. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al. (2022)</a></cite> proposed PC methods for samplers that take advantage of the rate change matrices of the continuous-time Markov chain (CTMC) formulation of discrete diffusion processes, but such samplers are known to perform worse than ancestral samplers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schiff et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> to address these challenges, which expands the design space of USDMs using non-Markovian <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">superposition posteriors</span> (or as we refer to them in this paper, <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-posteriors). These posteriors align with the intermediate marginals of discrete diffusion processes and give rise to <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers with predictor-corrector capabilities that are crucial for improving sample quality. In addition, <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> introduces an efficient curriculum learning strategy that advances the approach of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025a)</a></cite> by accelerating training and reducing memory usage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our contributions are threefold: (1) we propose a family of non-Markovian posteriors (<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-posteriors) for discrete diffusion with arbitrary noise priors that share the same marginals as the Markovian discrete diffusion process (Sec. <a href="#S3" title="3 The Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). (2) We demonstrate that the induced <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers improve text and image generation quality and scale better than standard ancestral samplers in high NFE regimes, closing the performance gap with respect to MDMs coupled with remasking samplers in high NFE regimes for text generation (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Ψ-Samplers ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>) and surpassing them on image generation tasks (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS1.SSS2" title="5.1.2 Image Modeling ‣ 5.1 Ψ-Samplers ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1.2</span></a>). (3) We reformulate the curriculum learning strategy proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, achieving a
2<math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup while reducing peak memory usage by 33% and end-to-end training time by 25%, while maintaining similar perplexity (Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, right, Table <a href="#A4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ D.3 Training Efficiency of Our Fast Curriculum ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental results ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and downstream task accuracy (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Step 2: Approximating the softmax ‣ 4 Scalable Curriculum for Faster Training ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}:=\{\mathbf{v}\in\{0,1\}^{K}:\sum_{i=1}^{K}\mathbf{v}_{i}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}:=\{\mathbf{v}\in\{0,1\}^{K}:\sum_{i=1}^{K}\mathbf{v}_{i}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of one-hot encodings of discrete random variables over <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> categories. Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\in\mathcal{V}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\in\mathcal{V}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a sequence of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> discrete variables in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the entry <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use boldface to denote both individual vectors and sequences; the context will make clear whether a symbol refers to a vector or a sequence. Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> simplex. For <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}\in\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}\in\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Cat}(\cdot;\mathbf{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Cat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Cat}(\cdot;\mathbf{v})</annotation></semantics></math> denote a categorical distribution such that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(\mathbf{u}_{i}=1)=\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(\mathbf{u}_{i}=1)=\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}\sim\text{Cat}(\cdot;\mathbf{v}),\mathbf{u}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Cat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}\sim\text{Cat}(\cdot;\mathbf{v}),\mathbf{u}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}\odot\mathbf{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}\odot\mathbf{b}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the dot and Hadamard products between two vectors respectively. Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{1}}=\{1\}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{1}}=\{1\}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the all-ones vector. Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}\in\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}\in\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> be a designated categorical distribution referred to as the prior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Discrete Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the clean data sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from the data distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Discrete diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2023</a>)</cite> define a sequence of increasingly noisy distributions <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{t})_{t\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{t})_{t\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math>, interpolating from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> to a factorized prior distribution, which is a product of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> independent <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{Cat}(.;\bm{\pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Cat</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>;</mo><mi>𝝅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Cat}(.;\bm{\pi})</annotation></semantics></math> distributions, using Markovian transitions defined independently across input dimensions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schiff et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{t}\sim\prod_{\ell=1}^{L}{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">∼</mo><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{t}\sim\prod_{\ell=1}^{L}{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the intermediate latents (sequence) at time step <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. This work focuses on factorized, interpolating noise processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, whose conditional marginal distribution takes the form:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\sim{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell};{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}})=\text{Cat}(.;{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}{\mathbf{x}}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}+(1-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}})\bm{\pi})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>Cat</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>;</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\sim{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell};{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}})=\text{Cat}(.;{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}{\mathbf{x}}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}+(1-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}})\bm{\pi}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is monotonically decreasing with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and is known as the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">noise schedule</span>. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) defines the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">forward process</span>, which progressively corrupts the data.
The goal is to learn a <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generative process</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p_{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p_{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterized by a neural network with parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, that reverses this forward process to map from the noise prior back to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The model is typically trained by minimizing the “Negative Evidence Lower Bound” (NELBO). The choice of token prior <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to two popular variants: Masked Diffusion Models (MDMs) and Uniform-state Diffusion Models (USDMs), which we discuss in the following.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.1 </span>Masked Diffusion Processes</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MDMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2025</a>)</cite> use a masked prior, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}={\mathbf{m}}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}={\mathbf{m}}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is the one-hot representation of a special [<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">mask</span>] token <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. During the forward process (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), tokens either remain unchanged or transition to the masked state <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>, after which they stay masked. This behavior carries over to the reverse process. The posterior of the reverse process <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\text{MDM}}_{s|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mtext>MDM</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\text{MDM}}_{s|t}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq s&lt;t&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq s&lt;t&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> can be derived using Bayes’ Rule, and is given by:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle q^{\text{MDM}}_{s|t}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\begin{cases}\text{Cat}\left(.;\frac{\alpha_{s}-\alpha_{t}}{1-\alpha_{t}}{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+\frac{1-\alpha_{s}}{1-\alpha_{t}}{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\right)&amp;\text{if ${\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}={\mathbf{m}}$,}\\
\text{Cat}(.;{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mtext>MDM</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>Cat</mtext><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>Cat</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q^{\text{MDM}}_{s|t}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\begin{cases}\text{Cat}\left(.;\frac{\alpha_{s}-\alpha_{t}}{1-\alpha_{t}}{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+\frac{1-\alpha_{s}}{1-\alpha_{t}}{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\right)&amp;\text{if ${\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}={\mathbf{m}}$,}\\
\text{Cat}(.;{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The approximate reverse posterior is <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{p^{\theta}_{s|t}}=\prod_{\ell}q^{\text{MDM}}_{s|t}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}={\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}1:L}},t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mtext>MDM</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#787878" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878">L</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p^{\theta}_{s|t}}=\prod_{\ell}q^{\text{MDM}}_{s|t}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}={\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}1:L}},t))</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\theta}:\mathcal{V}^{L}\times[0,1]\to\Delta^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\theta}:\mathcal{V}^{L}\times[0,1]\to\Delta^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the denoising model.
A key limitation is that once unmasked, tokens cannot be remasked (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1.1 Masked Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). This can create compounding errors during inference, as the denoising model <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> imperfectly models the clean data.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Predictor-Corrector Methods</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite> propose posteriors, and associated samplers (ReMDM) that maintain the same marginals as (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1.1 Masked Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) during the generation process, while allowing remasking and generalizing previous training-free predictor-corrector methods such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.2 </span>Uniform-state Diffusion Processes</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, discrete diffusion models can use a uniform prior <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}={\bm{1}}/K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}={\bm{1}}/K</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schiff et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. This choice allows tokens to change values multiple times throughout the generative process, in contrast to Masked diffusion. This property allows USDMs to excel in few-step generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> and guidance applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schiff et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">USDMs admit the following posterior distribution <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\text{USDM}}_{s|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mtext>USDM</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\text{USDM}}_{s|t}</annotation></semantics></math> (for brevity, we simply write <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}{q_{s|t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">s</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}{q_{s|t}}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\text{USDM}}_{s|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mtext>USDM</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\text{USDM}}_{s|t}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}{q_{s|t}}}(.\mid{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\text{Cat}\left(.;\frac{K{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\odot{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+({\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t|s}}-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}){\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+(\alpha_{s}-\alpha_{t}){\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+(1-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t|s}})(1-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{s}}){\bm{1}}/K}{K{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}\langle{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\rangle+1-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">s</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>Cat</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}{q_{s|t}}}(.\mid{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\text{Cat}\left(.;\frac{K{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\odot{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+({\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t|s}}-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}){\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+(\alpha_{s}-\alpha_{t}){\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+(1-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t|s}})(1-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{s}}){\bm{1}}/K}{K{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}\langle{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\rangle+1-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This posterior induces the following NELBO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{NELBO}\left({\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q},p_{\theta};{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>NELBO</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{NELBO}\left({\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q},p_{\theta};{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\,{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}};\alpha_{t})}\;\sum_{\ell\in[L]}\textit{f}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},t),{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}};{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">f</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\,{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}};\alpha_{t})}\;\sum_{\ell\in[L]}\textit{f}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},t),{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}};{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textit{f}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},t)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">f</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textit{f}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}};{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\frac{{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}^{\prime}}{K{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}}\Bigg[\frac{K}{\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{r}}-\frac{K}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})_{r}}-\left(\zeta_{t}\mathds{1}_{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}={\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}+\mathds{1}_{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\neq{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}\right)\sum_{j}\log\frac{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{\theta})_{r}}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{\theta})_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mmultiscripts><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>K</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>r</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}};{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\frac{{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}^{\prime}}{K{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}}\Bigg[\frac{K}{\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{r}}-\frac{K}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})_{r}}-\left(\zeta_{t}\mathds{1}_{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}={\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}+\mathds{1}_{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\neq{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}\right)\sum_{j}\log\frac{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{\theta})_{r}}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{\theta})_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle-K\frac{{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}}{1-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}}\log\frac{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{\theta})_{r}}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{\theta})_{i}}\mathds{1}_{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\neq{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}-\left((K-1)\zeta_{t}\mathds{1}_{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}={\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}-\frac{1}{\zeta_{t}}\mathds{1}_{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\neq{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}\right)\log\zeta_{t}\Bigg]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.167em">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-K\frac{{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}}{1-{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}}\log\frac{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{\theta})_{r}}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{\theta})_{i}}\mathds{1}_{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\neq{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}-\left((K-1)\zeta_{t}\mathds{1}_{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}={\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}-\frac{1}{\zeta_{t}}\mathds{1}_{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\neq{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}\right)\log\zeta_{t}\Bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}=K\alpha_{t}{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+(1-\alpha_{t}){\bm{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}=K\alpha_{t}{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+(1-\alpha_{t}){\bm{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{\theta}=K\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t},t)+(1-\alpha_{t}){\bm{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{\theta}=K\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t},t)+(1-\alpha_{t}){\bm{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the time derivative of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\arg\max_{j\in[K]}({\mathbf{z}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{t})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\arg\max_{j\in[K]}({\mathbf{z}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{t})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the nonzero entry of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}=\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{K\alpha_{t}+1-\alpha_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}=\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{K\alpha_{t}+1-\alpha_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the index in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to 1, that is, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Diffusion Duality</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025a)</a></cite> show that USDMs emerge from an underlying Gaussian diffusion process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2023</a>)</cite> on the one-hot representation <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Gaussian diffusion begins with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math> and progressively adds Gaussian noise leading to a sequence of noisy latents <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\in\mathbb{R}^{K}\sim{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{q}_{t}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\in\mathbb{R}^{K}\sim{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{q}_{t}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with the marginals:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{q}_{t}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}};{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}})={\mathcal{N}}(.;{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},(1-{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}})\mathbf{I}_{K})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{q}_{t}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}};{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}})={\mathcal{N}}(.;{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},(1-{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}})\mathbf{I}_{K}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}})_{t\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}})_{t\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> is a monotonically decreasing noise schedule. Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}:\mathbb{R}^{K}\to\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}:\mathbb{R}^{K}\to\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> map a continuous vector <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> to the one-hot vector corresponding to the index of its largest entry in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}(\mathbf{v})={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}_{{\mathbf{z}}\in\mathcal{V}}{\mathbf{z}}^{\top}\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}(\mathbf{v})={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}_{{\mathbf{z}}\in\mathcal{V}}{\mathbf{z}}^{\top}\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. When applied to a sequence of Gaussian latents <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> transforms them to the discrete latents <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> whose marginals take the form:
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\sim{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}};{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#9A0000">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\sim{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}};{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}))</annotation></semantics></math>, where the function <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}:[0,1]\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#9A0000">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}:[0,1]\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>
is the <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion Transformation Operator</span>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}})=\frac{K}{K-1}\left[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\phi\left(z-\frac{{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}}{\sqrt{1-{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}}}}\right)\Phi^{K-1}(z)\text{d}z-\frac{1}{K}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#9A0000">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}})=\frac{K}{K-1}\left[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\phi\left(z-\frac{{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}}{\sqrt{1-{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}}}}\right)\Phi^{K-1}(z)\text{d}z-\frac{1}{K}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi(z)=\exp(-z^{2}/2)/\sqrt{2\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(z)=\exp(-z^{2}/2)/\sqrt{2\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(z)=\int_{-\infty}^{z}\phi(t){\text{d}}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi>z</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(z)=\int_{-\infty}^{z}\phi(t){\text{d}}t</annotation></semantics></math> are the standard Normal PDF and CDF, respectively.
More formally, this relationship is expressed as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}};{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}))=[{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}]_{\scalebox{0.6}{★}}{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{q}_{t}}({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}};{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#9A0000">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:4.1pt;height:3.7pt;vertical-align:-0.8pt;" depth="0.8pt" height="2.9pt" width="4.1pt"><mtext>★</mtext></mpadded></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}};{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}))=[{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}]_{\scalebox{0.6}{★}}{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{q}_{t}}({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}};{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where the 
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:5.7pt;height:5.4pt;vertical-align:-1.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-1.9pt,1.8pt) scale(0.6,0.6) ;">
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.4.1.1" class="ltx_p">★</span>
</span></span> operator denotes the <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pushforward</span> of the <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Gaussian density under the <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> map, yielding a categorical distribution with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> classes. Note that while the marginal distribution <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}|{\mathbf{x}};{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#9A0000">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{t}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t}|{\mathbf{x}};{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}))</annotation></semantics></math> matches the discrete-space marginal in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">this does not imply that the full trajectory <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}({\mathbf{w}}_{t})\}_{t\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\mathbf{z}}_{t}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}({\mathbf{w}}_{t})\}_{t\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> follows a (Markovian) discrete diffusion process</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. An interesting outcome of (<a href="#S2.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ The Diffusion Duality ‣ 2.1.2 Uniform-state Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is that the discrete NELBO (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2.1.2 Uniform-state Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) can be written in terms of Gaussian latents in the following manner, where the second <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied
to each token independently:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{NELBO}\left({\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q},p_{\theta};{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>NELBO</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{NELBO}\left({\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q},p_{\theta};{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},t\sim{\mathcal{U}}[0,1],{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{q}_{t}}}\sum_{\ell\in[L]}\textit{f}\Big({\mathbf{z}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{t}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}),\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}({\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}({{\mathbf{w}}_{t}}),t),{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}});{\mathbf{x}}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">f</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#9A0000">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},t\sim{\mathcal{U}}[0,1],{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{q}_{t}}}\sum_{\ell\in[L]}\textit{f}\Big({\mathbf{z}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{t}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}),\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}({\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}({{\mathbf{w}}_{t}}),t),{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}});{\mathbf{x}}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21185v1/psi-samplers.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/99;" width="548" height="99"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers combine predictor and corrector steps. The <span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BEDCFC;">predictor</span> transitions from <math id="S2.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S2.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{q_{s|t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{q_{s|t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, but fails to remask tokens in MDMs. The <span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F3E0E0;">corrector</span> steps inject noise via <math id="S2.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, to revise earlier predictions. For <math id="S2.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, noise injection enables error correction while preserving the forward
process marginals. Our framework extends prior PC methods  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to arbitrary priors <math id="S2.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Curriculum Learning</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Curriculum learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al., 2009</a>)</cite> trains models by gradually increasing task difficulty. Building on this idea, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025a)</a></cite> accelerate early training by using a biased but low-variance NELBO estimator for (<a href="#S2.Ex3" title="In The Diffusion Duality ‣ 2.1.2 Uniform-state Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). Concretely, during the first 50% of training steps, the hard <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> used to convert Gaussian latents into discrete tokens in the transformer’s input is replaced by a low-temperature softmax relaxation. This relaxation interfaces naturally with the transformer input layer: if the latent at position <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is a probability vector <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\in\Delta^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\in\Delta^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the token representation is computed as a matrix product <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\top}{\mathbf{y}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\top}{\mathbf{y}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vocabulary embedding matrix. For one-hot <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math> (as produced by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}</annotation></semantics></math>), this reduces to a standard embedding lookup; for softmax-relaxed latents, it becomes a linear combination of vocabulary embeddings. As a result, the model is no longer asked to denoise from a fully corrupted discrete token embedding, but instead receives an embedding that is a superposition of clean and noisy token embedding. This “partially clean” input provides a direct signal about the underlying token, making denoising easier than relying solely on the surrounding context. Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Intuitive Explanation ‣ 3 The Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (top) illustrates this curriculum. More formally, during the curriculum phase <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025a)</a></cite> optimize the following loss, where the <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathrm{softmax}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathrm{softmax}}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied independently at each position:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}^{\text{train}}=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},t\sim{\mathcal{U}}[\beta,\gamma],{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{q}_{t}}}\sum_{\ell\in[L]}\textit{f}\Big({\mathbf{z}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{t}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}({\mathbf{w}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{t}),\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathrm{softmax}}({{\mathbf{w}}_{t}/}{\tau}),t),{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}});{\mathbf{x}}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">f</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#9A0000">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}^{\text{train}}=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},t\sim{\mathcal{U}}[\beta,\gamma],{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{q}_{t}}}\sum_{\ell\in[L]}\textit{f}\Big({\mathbf{z}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{t}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}({\mathbf{w}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}_{t}),\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathrm{softmax}}({{\mathbf{w}}_{t}/}{\tau}),t),{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}}:={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}});{\mathbf{x}}^{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Notice that <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Curriculum Learning ‣ 2.1.2 Uniform-state Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) reduces to the NELBO (<a href="#S2.Ex3" title="In The Diffusion Duality ‣ 2.1.2 Uniform-state Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) in the limit <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\tau\to 0}{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathrm{softmax}}(\mathbf{v}/\tau)={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}(\mathbf{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\tau\to 0}{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathrm{softmax}}(\mathbf{v}/\tau)={\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\argmax}(\mathbf{v})</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jang et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maddison et al. (2017)</a></cite>.
However, explicitly materializing the high-dimensional latents <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is memory-intensive, an issue we address in Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4 Scalable Curriculum for Faster Training ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Diffusion Guidance</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For continuous data, diffusion models have achieved state-of-the-art controllable generation through both classifier-based guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>)</cite> and Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nichol and Dhariwal (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans (2022)</a></cite>). These approaches have since been extended to discrete data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gruver et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{1,\dots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{1,\dots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote one of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> possible classes. For CFG, the sampling posterior <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p_{\theta}}^{(\gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p_{\theta}}^{(\gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which modulates the strength of the guidance term via the temperature parameter <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nisonoff et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schiff et al., 2025</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log{p_{\theta}}^{(\gamma)}({\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\mid y,{\mathbf{z}}_{t})=\gamma\log{p_{\theta}}({\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\mid y,{\mathbf{z}}_{t})+(1-\gamma)\log{p_{\theta}}({\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\mid\emptyset,{\mathbf{z}}_{t});\;\forall\ell\in[L]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>s</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>s</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>s</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log{p_{\theta}}^{(\gamma)}({\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\mid y,{\mathbf{z}}_{t})=\gamma\log{p_{\theta}}({\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\mid y,{\mathbf{z}}_{t})+(1-\gamma)\log{p_{\theta}}({\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}\mid\emptyset,{\mathbf{z}}_{t});\;\forall\ell\in[L],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> denotes no class conditioning, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p_{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p_{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the generative posterior (Sec. <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-Posteriors</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multiple joint distributions can give rise to the same marginals as the discrete diffusion process defined in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). In this work, we introduce a family of posteriors, denoted <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Psi}</annotation></semantics></math>, that share the same marginals as in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>); see Suppl. <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Proof that the Ψ-posteriors have the correct marginals ‣ Appendix A Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> for details. <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">These alternative generative processes are non-Markovian and apply both to the Masked diffusion processes and to the Uniform-state diffusion processes</span>.
Specifically, we define the posteriors for the generative process as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Psi_{s|t}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\kappa_{t}{{q_{s|t}}}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})+(1-\kappa_{t}){\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{s}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}});\;\forall\ell\in[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Psi_{s|t}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\kappa_{t}{{q_{s|t}}}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})+(1-\kappa_{t}){\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{s}}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}});\;\forall\ell\in[L]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Psi_{1}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\text{Cat}(.|{\mathbf{\bm{\pi}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>Cat</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝝅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{1}(.|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\text{Cat}(.|{\mathbf{\bm{\pi}}})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}={\mathbf{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}={\mathbf{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> for MDMs and <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}={\bm{1}}/K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}={\bm{1}}/K</annotation></semantics></math> for USDMs. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ 3 The Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) is thus a linear combination of the forward process (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and the reverse posteriors (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1.1 Masked Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2.1.2 Uniform-state Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) of standard discrete diffusion models. We therefore refer to these as <span id="S3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">superposition posteriors</span>, or simply <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-posteriors.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-Forward Processes</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the interpolating diffusion process in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) discretized into <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps. Let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{t(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{t(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the latent variables at times <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{t(i)}=i/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{t(i)}=i/T</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq T</annotation></semantics></math>. The distribution of a trajectory <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{0:1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{0:1}</annotation></semantics></math> factorizes independently over tokens as: <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Psi({\mathbf{z}}_{0:1}|{\mathbf{x}})=\prod_{\ell}\Psi({\mathbf{z}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}_{0:1}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi({\mathbf{z}}_{0:1}|{\mathbf{x}})=\prod_{\ell}\Psi({\mathbf{z}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}_{0:1}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Psi({\mathbf{z}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}_{0:1}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\Psi_{1}({\mathbf{z}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}_{1}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})\prod_{i=1}^{T}\Psi_{s|t}({\mathbf{z}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}_{s(i)}|{\mathbf{z}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}_{t(i)},{\mathbf{x}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi({\mathbf{z}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}_{0:1}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\Psi_{1}({\mathbf{z}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}_{1}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})\prod_{i=1}^{T}\Psi_{s|t}({\mathbf{z}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}_{s(i)}|{\mathbf{z}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}}_{t(i)},{\mathbf{x}}^{{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}})</annotation></semantics></math>. In what follows, we use <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,t</annotation></semantics></math> as shorthand for <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{s(i)},{t(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{s(i)},{t(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
The forward process can be derived from Bayes’ rule: <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\Psi({\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})\Psi({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})/\Psi({\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>s</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>s</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>s</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})=\Psi({\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})\Psi({\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})/\Psi({\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}|{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Unlike the Markovian interpolating process in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), this forward process is generally not Markovian, since each <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math> may depend on both <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>s</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{s}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-Reverse Processes</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Suppl. <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Approximate Reverse Marginals ‣ Appendix A Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>, we show that the approximate reverse posterior takes the form:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle[\Psi^{\theta}_{s|t}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t})]^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}=\kappa_{t}{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}{q_{s|t}}}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t},t))+(1-\kappa_{t})\left[\alpha_{s}{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{0|t}}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t},t))+(1-\alpha_{s})\bm{\pi}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">s</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">q</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[\Psi^{\theta}_{s|t}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t})]^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}=\kappa_{t}{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}{q_{s|t}}}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t},t))+(1-\kappa_{t})\left[\alpha_{s}{\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}q_{0|t}}(.|{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t},t))+(1-\alpha_{s})\bm{\pi}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the denoising model. We dub (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Ψ-Reverse Processes ‣ 3 The Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) as <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-sampler. For <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\kappa_{t}=1)_{t\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\kappa_{t}=1)_{t\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math>, we recover the standard ancestral sampler defined in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1.1 Masked Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) for MDMs and (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2.1.2 Uniform-state Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) for USDMs. Notice that for <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{s|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{s|t}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a noisier version of the ancestral sampler marginal <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{q_{s|t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{q_{s|t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is analogous to Predictor-Corrector methods in Gaussian diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, where the corrector introduces additional Gaussian noise. In our case, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> plays the role of the corrector, while <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{q_{s|t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{q_{s|t}}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as the predictor.
The <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-posteriors also admit a principled NELBO formulation (see Suppl. <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Negative Evidence Lower Bound ‣ Appendix A Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>), though this is not directly relevant for sampling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Corollary</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}={\mathbf{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}={\mathbf{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>, different choices of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\kappa_{t}\}_{t\in[0,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\kappa_{t}\}_{t\in[0,1]}</annotation></semantics></math> recover previous Predictor-Corrector formulations in the literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> (see Suppl. <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Recovering Predictor-Corrector Methods for Masked Diffusion ‣ Appendix A Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> for the proof). The <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> framework thus subsumes these samplers as special cases, extending these predictor-corrector methods for discrete diffusion with any prior <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="127.73" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 127.73" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,127.73) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000B3;" fill="#0000B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 121.83 C 0 125.09 2.64 127.73 5.91 127.73 L 544.09 127.73 C 547.36 127.73 550 125.09 550 121.83 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E0E0FF;" fill="#E0E0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 121.83 C 1.97 124 3.73 125.77 5.91 125.77 L 544.09 125.77 C 546.27 125.77 548.03 124 548.03 121.83 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.75 11.36)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.2em;--ltx-fo-height:7.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="109.01" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 106.32)" width="528.58"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.2em;">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 1:<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math></span>-samplers generalize prior Predictor-Corrector methods to arbitrary noise priors<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, subsuming ReMDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as special cases.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Intuitive Explanation</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, the denoiser <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> imperfectly models the clean data <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The key to the effectiveness of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-sampler is the offset term <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\kappa_{t})(1-\alpha_{s})\bm{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝝅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\kappa_{t})(1-\alpha_{s})\bm{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Ψ-Reverse Processes ‣ 3 The Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), which enables error correction during generation.
For MDMs (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}={\mathbf{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}={\mathbf{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>), this offset allows previously denoised tokens to return to the masked state, unlike the ancestral sampler, which prevents remasking (see Sec. <a href="#S2.SS1.SSS1" title="2.1.1 Masked Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1.1</span></a>). Incorrect tokens can thus be replaced with better ones.
For USDMs (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}={\bm{1}}/K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}={\bm{1}}/K</annotation></semantics></math>), the offset ensures every token has non-zero sampling probability. Even if the denoiser assigns near-zero probability to the correct token, the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-sampler gives it a chance to appear, whereas ancestral sampling would not.
While this offset may occasionally introduce incorrect tokens, the marginals of the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers (<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ 3 The Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) match those of the Markovian forward process (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), hence we converge to the correct distribution given sufficient samples.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21185v1/efficient_curriculum.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/195;" width="548" height="195" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficient Curriculum for USDMs.</span> Duo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> replaces discrete lookups with linear combinations of all <math id="S3.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> embeddings: (1) Gaussian diffusion on one-hot representations, (2) Low-temperature <math id="S3.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathrm{softmax}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathrm{softmax}}</annotation></semantics></math>, (3) weighted sum. <math id="S3.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> exploits the sparsity of the tempered softmax (most weights are effectively zero), and simulate the k largest entries (out of K) using ordered statistics. The approximate normalizer <math id="S3.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\tilde{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\tilde{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a closed form expression (<a href="#S4.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Step 2: Approximating the softmax ‣ 4 Scalable Curriculum for Faster Training ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). <math id="S3.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> has a 33% lower memory and 25% faster training than Duo.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Scalable Curriculum for Faster Training</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from Sec. <a href="#S2.SS1.SSS2.Px2" title="Curriculum Learning ‣ 2.1.2 Uniform-state Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1.2</span></a> that the curriculum of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025a)</a></cite> accelerates training by replacing discrete tokens as inputs to the transformer with softmaxed Gaussian latents. Naively, however, this requires materializing a <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional weight vector for every token at every training step, which is infeasible for modern LLM vocabularies with <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K&gt;100{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>100,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K&gt;100{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite>.
Our key observation is that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025a)</a></cite> use a very low temperature <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.Ex3" title="In The Diffusion Duality ‣ 2.1.2 Uniform-state Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). At such temperatures, the softmax concentrates almost all its mass on only a few entries, making most of the <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> weights negligible. We exploit this induced sparsity by approximating the full linear combination using only <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\ll K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\ll K</annotation></semantics></math> embeddings (Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Intuitive Explanation ‣ 3 The Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, bottom). We illustrate the process hereby:</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 1: Generating top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> entries in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math> w/o materializing it</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in[K]</annotation></semantics></math> be the nonzero coordinate of the one-hot vector <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})_{o}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})_{o}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}={\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}={\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}{\mathbf{x}}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}}+{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}\bm{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}=\sqrt{1-{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}=\sqrt{1-{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Therefore, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})_{o}\sim{\mathcal{N}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}},{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})_{o}\sim{\mathcal{N}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}},{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})_{i\neq o}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})_{i\neq o}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
All coordinates in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]\setminus{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]\setminus{o}</annotation></semantics></math> are exchangeable (i.i.d. with mean <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>), while the coordinate <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> is the only one with a shifted mean. As a result, the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> set falls into one of two cases: <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;">Case 1: <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> is not among the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so all <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> winners lie in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]\setminus{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]\setminus{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;">Case 2: <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> is among the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so the winners are <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{o}</annotation></semantics></math> plus <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> indices from <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]\setminus{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]\setminus{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we describe how to sample the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values (and later their indices) without ever forming the full <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=K{-}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=K{-}1</annotation></semantics></math> and consider <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}_{1},\dots,\tilde{w}_{m}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}_{1},\dots,\tilde{w}_{m}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Rather than drawing all <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> values, we exploit the fact that order statistics of i.i.d. <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uniform</em> random variables can be sampled recursively: the maximum of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> variables has CDF <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, so it can be sampled directly. Conditioned on the maximum, the remaining values are also i.i.d. uniforms, so the next-largest can be sampled the same way, and so on. Applying the inverse normal CDF <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{-1}(\cdot)\cdot{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{-1}(\cdot)\cdot{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> converts the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> uniform order statistics into the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> Gaussian values (see Suppl. <a href="#A2.SS1.SSS4" title="B.1.4 Generating the 𝑘 largest Gaussian random variables out of 𝐾 ‣ B.1 Sampling the top-𝑘 values in 𝐰_𝑡^ℓ Without Materializing The Full Vector ‣ Appendix B Fast Curriculum ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1.4</span></a> for details). We denote the result
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" alttext="\mathcal{K}=(\mathcal{K}_{1}\geq\dots\geq\mathcal{K}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">𝒦</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">𝒦</mi><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">≥</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD" mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">≥</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">𝒦</mi><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">k</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}=(\mathcal{K}_{1}\geq\dots\geq\mathcal{K}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></span>, where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th largest among the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{-}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{-}1</annotation></semantics></math> zero-mean coordinates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We draw independently the “special” entry <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}\sim{\mathcal{N}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}},{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}\sim{\mathcal{N}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}},{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which matches the distribution of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now compare <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}</annotation></semantics></math> to the current <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th largest value among the zero-mean coordinates:
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;">If <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{k}&gt;\tilde{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">𝒦</mi><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">&gt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">w</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{k}&gt;\tilde{w}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span> then <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> cannot enter the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;">We are in Case 1, and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> already contains the correct top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values.</span> <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;">If <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}&gt;\mathcal{K}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">w</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">~</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">&gt;</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">𝒦</mi><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}&gt;\mathcal{K}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> must be in the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. We are in Case 2.</span> Let
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\big|\{j\in[k]:\mathcal{K}_{j}&gt;\tilde{w}\}\big|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\big|\{j\in[k]:\mathcal{K}_{j}&gt;\tilde{w}\}\big|</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of values in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> that are larger than <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. We insert <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}</annotation></semantics></math> at rank <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math> and drop the previous smallest value:
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" alttext="\mathcal{K}\leftarrow\mathcal{K}_{1:r}\|\tilde{w}\|\mathcal{K}_{r+1:k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">𝒦</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">𝒦</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">r</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">w</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">~</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">𝒦</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}\leftarrow\mathcal{K}_{1:r}\|\tilde{w}\|\mathcal{K}_{r+1:k-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes concatenation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For clarity, let <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{S}{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{S}{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of all possible tuples with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> distinct elements in the set <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. We generate the index tuple <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;">Case 1:</span> all top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> indices lie in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]\setminus{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]\setminus{o}</annotation></semantics></math>; hence,
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" alttext="\mathcal{I}\sim\binom{[K]\setminus\{o\}}{k}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">ℐ</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">∼</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">K</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">∖</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">o</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">k</mi></mfrac><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0E0BD;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#F0E0BD">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}\sim\binom{[K]\setminus\{o\}}{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;">Case 2:</span> the index <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> appears at position <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Sample the indices for the remaining <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> values from <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K]\setminus{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K]\setminus{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, split into those above and below <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math>:
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" alttext="\mathcal{I}=L\|(o)\|R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">ℐ</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">o</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=L\|(o)\|R</annotation></semantics></math></span>,
where
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" alttext="L\sim{\binom{[K]\setminus\{o\}}{r}},R\sim\binom{[K]\setminus\{o\}\setminus L}{k-r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">∼</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">K</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">∖</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">o</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">r</mi></mfrac><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">R</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">∼</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">K</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">∖</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">o</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">∖</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;" mathbackground="#A3D4B0">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\sim{\binom{[K]\setminus\{o\}}{r}},R\sim\binom{[K]\setminus\{o\}\setminus L}{k-r-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;">.</span></span>
This produces both the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values and their matching indices while sampling only <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(k)</annotation></semantics></math> random variables, without constructing the full <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 2: Approximating the softmax</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values and indices <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{K},\mathcal{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{K},\mathcal{I})</annotation></semantics></math> from Step 1, we approximate the
softmax-weighted embedding vector by retaining only the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> selected rows of the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{embeddings}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">embeddings</mtext><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{embeddings}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the embedding size):</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathrm{softmax}}({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})^{\top}\texttt{embeddings}\approx\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{\exp(\mathcal{K}_{i}/\tau)}{\tilde{Z}}\texttt{embeddings}[{\mathcal{I}_{i}}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" mathcolor="#9A0000">softmax</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#787878;" mathcolor="#787878" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">embeddings</mtext></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">embeddings</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathrm{softmax}}({\mathbf{w}}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0.4688,0.4688,0.4688}\ell}})^{\top}\texttt{embeddings}\approx\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{\exp(\mathcal{K}_{i}/\tau)}{\tilde{Z}}\texttt{embeddings}[{\mathcal{I}_{i}}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{embeddings}[j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">embeddings</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{embeddings}[j]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th row.
The normalizer <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> includes both sampled (top-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>) and unsampled terms. While each unsampled term is small, their sum may be non-negligible, hence we approximate it as (Suppl. <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Approximating the Weighted Sum of the Embeddings ‣ Appendix B Fast Curriculum ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{Z}\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{Z}\approx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{\sum_{i=1}^{k}\exp\left(\frac{\mathcal{K}_{i}}{\tau}\right)}_{\text{top-$k$ terms}}+\underbrace{\delta\exp\left(\frac{\tilde{w}}{\tau}\right)}_{\text{clean token}}+\underbrace{(K-k-\delta)\exp\left(\frac{{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}}}{2\tau^{2}}-\log\Phi\!\left(\frac{\mathcal{K}_{k}}{{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}}\right)+\log\Phi\!\left(\frac{\mathcal{K}_{k}-{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}}/\tau}{{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}}\right)\right)}_{\text{unsampled zero-mean terms}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>τ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mtext>top-</mtext><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mtext> terms</mtext></mrow></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>clean token</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>unsampled zero-mean terms</mtext></munder></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{\sum_{i=1}^{k}\exp\left(\frac{\mathcal{K}_{i}}{\tau}\right)}_{\text{top-$k$ terms}}+\underbrace{\delta\exp\left(\frac{\tilde{w}}{\tau}\right)}_{\text{clean token}}+\underbrace{(K-k-\delta)\exp\left(\frac{{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}}}{2\tau^{2}}-\log\Phi\!\left(\frac{\mathcal{K}_{k}}{{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}}\right)+\log\Phi\!\left(\frac{\mathcal{K}_{k}-{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}2}}}/\tau}{{\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}}\right)\right)}_{\text{unsampled zero-mean terms}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}\in\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}\in\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#A3D4B0;">case 2</span>) and 0 otherwise.
We provide the full derivation in Suppl. <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Approximating the Weighted Sum of the Embeddings ‣ Appendix B Fast Curriculum ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> and pseudocode in Algo. <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ B.4 Implementation of the Fast Curriculum ‣ Appendix B Fast Curriculum ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, the curriculum objective in (<a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Curriculum Learning ‣ 2.1.2 Uniform-state Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) requires evaluating the diffusion transformation operator <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#9A0000">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. Directly computing <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#9A0000">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> via (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ The Diffusion Duality ‣ 2.1.2 Uniform-state Diffusion Processes ‣ 2.1 Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is prohibitively expensive during training; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025a)</a></cite> therefore precompute and cache many <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}},{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0B5396;" mathcolor="#0B5396">t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#9A0000">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#E69138;" mathcolor="#E69138">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\color[rgb]{0.043,0.3242,0.5898}\alpha_{t}},{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}({\color[rgb]{0.9023,0.5703,0.2188}\tilde{\alpha}_{t}}))</annotation></semantics></math> pairs, which is cumbersome. Instead, we compute <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#9A0000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#9A0000">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.6055,0,0}\mathcal{T}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> on the fly using its Taylor expansion; see Suppl. <a href="#A2.SS3.SSS1" title="B.3.1 Series Representation of 𝒯 and ∂_𝑡𝒯 ‣ B.3 Efficient computation of 𝒯 During Training ‣ Appendix B Fast Curriculum ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S4.T1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy on multiple-choice question answering datasets.</span> Abbreviations: Arc-e (ARC-Easy), Arc-c (ARC-Challenge), HSwag (HellaSwag), WinoG (Winogrande), PIQA (Physical Intelligence Question Answering), OQA (OpenBookQA). <sup id="S4.T1.8" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>Results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deschenaux et al. (2025)</a></cite>. <math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>) achieves slightly higher accuracy than Duo on 4 out of 6 tasks. Overall, <math id="S4.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> matches Duo’s performance while using 25% fewer flops</span>. The highest accuracy among USDMs is <span id="S4.T1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bolded</span>. The absolute best per column is <span id="S4.T1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>.</figcaption>
