</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers on language modeling (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS1.SSS1" title="5.1.1 Language Modeling ‣ 5.1 Ψ-Samplers ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1.1</span></a>) and image generation (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS1.SSS2" title="5.1.2 Image Modeling ‣ 5.1 Ψ-Samplers ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1.2</span></a>), showing that <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers markedly improve text and image quality, making USDMs outperform MDMs in sample quality. In Sec. <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Fast Curriculum ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we show that <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> matches Duo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> while using 33% less memory and training 25% faster, enabled by our efficient curriculum strategy (Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4 Scalable Curriculum for Faster Training ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span><math id="S5.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-Samplers</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1 </span>Language Modeling</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="163.56" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 163.56" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,163.56) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000B3;" fill="#0000B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 157.65 C 0 160.91 2.64 163.56 5.91 163.56 L 544.09 163.56 C 547.36 163.56 550 160.91 550 157.65 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E0E0FF;" fill="#E0E0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 157.65 C 1.97 159.83 3.73 161.59 5.91 161.59 L 544.09 161.59 C 546.27 161.59 548.03 159.83 548.03 157.65 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.75 11.36)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.2em;--ltx-fo-height:10.27em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="144.83" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 142.14)" width="528.58"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.2em;">
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 2:<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math></span>-samplers substantially improve Generative Perplexity<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> for USDMs, with gains especially pronounced when NFEs exceed the sequence length.</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 3:<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Unlike ancestral sampling, which plateaus, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math></span>-samplers continue to improve with more sampling steps<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, closing the gap with Masked diffusion models.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental Settings</h5>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:25%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21185v1/profile_and_kappa.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:137/103;" width="137" height="103"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Illustration of the possible evolution of <math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and the associated <math id="S5.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, we use <math id="S5.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> close to 1 during the PC phase.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare MDLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and ReMDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers. We use the original checkpoints of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2024)</a></cite>, trained for 1M steps with a batch size of 512 on OpenWebText (OWT; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gokaslan and Cohen (2019)</a></cite>) and context length <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=1024</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained with the same context length, batch size and number of steps, but with the efficient curriculum.
Refer to the original works for more details. We measure the sample quality using the Gen. PPL (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) computed with GPT-2 Large <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and the diversity using the unigram entropy (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dieleman et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. We cast logits to 64-bit precision for sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. See Suppl. <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Ψ-samplers ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a> for more details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results and Ablation</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (left) shows the Gen. PPL and entropy as a function of the NFE. <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers outperforms MDLM with ReMDM and ancestral sampling across the entire range of NFEs. As the number of NFEs increases beyond the sequence length, ReMDM and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers further improve sample quality while ancestral sampling plateaus. We ablate on the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> schedule type (cap, rescale, loop, see Suppl. <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 ReMDM Sampling Schedules ‣ Appendix A Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>), the step-size parameter <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, the nucleus sampling threshold <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\{0.9,0.95,1.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.95</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\{0.9,0.95,1.0\}</annotation></semantics></math> (Suppl. <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Tuning 𝜅_𝑡 for the Ψ-samplers ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental results ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>). The rescale schedule with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> yields the best Gen. PPL while preserving the unigram entropy. Nucleus sampling (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{=}0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{=}0.9</annotation></semantics></math>) consistently improves Gen. PPL for both MDLM and Duo, as observed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">How to Pick <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>?</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recommend the ReMDM-equivalent rescale schedule with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> and nucleus sampling (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>), using the log-linear noise schedule with linearly decreasing <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, which outperforms the “loop” strategy (Suppl. <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 ReMDM Sampling Schedules ‣ Appendix A Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2 </span>Image Modeling</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="125.04" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 125.04" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,125.04) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000B3;" fill="#0000B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 119.14 C 0 122.4 2.64 125.04 5.91 125.04 L 544.09 125.04 C 547.36 125.04 550 122.4 550 119.14 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E0E0FF;" fill="#E0E0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 119.14 C 1.97 121.31 3.73 123.08 5.91 123.08 L 544.09 123.08 C 546.27 123.08 548.03 121.31 548.03 119.14 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.75 11.36)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.2em;--ltx-fo-height:7.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="106.32" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 103.62)" width="528.58"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.2em;">
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 4:<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> </span>On CIFAR-10, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathcolor="#000000">Duo</mtext><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers achieves better FID and IS<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> than MDLM with both ancestral sampling and ReMDM.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental Setup</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train the same 35M-parameter U-Net as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al. (2023)</a></cite> on CIFAR-10 for 1.5M steps. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schiff et al. (2025)</a></cite>, the U-Net is made class conditional and we sample with Discrete Classifier-free Guidance (CFG; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schiff et al. (2025)</a></cite>). See Suppl. <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Ψ-samplers ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a> for full training details. We report the <em id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fréchet Inception Distance</em> (FID; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heusel et al. (2018)</a></cite>) and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Inception Score</span> (IS; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salimans et al. (2016)</a></cite>) between the training set and generated samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results and Ablation</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(right)</span> and Fig. <a href="#A4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ D.1 Tuning 𝜅_𝑡 for the Ψ-samplers ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental results ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> show that <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers and ReMDM substantially improve FID and IS compared to ancestral sampling, with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> reaching the best scores overall. We ablate on the sampling noise schedule (cosine vs. log-linear), <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the activation range <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{\text{off}},t_{\text{on}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{\text{off}},t_{\text{on}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, nucleus sampling, and the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> schedules (including the ReMDM variants; see Suppl. <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 ReMDM Sampling Schedules ‣ Appendix A Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>). Full results are in Suppl. <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Tuning 𝜅_𝑡 for the Ψ-samplers ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental results ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>. Using <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> close to 1 (light noise injection) with a cosine schedule achieves the best FID and IS, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> tolerates stronger noise injection than MDLM. With ancestral sampling, nucleus sampling improves the FID in the low NFE regime for MDLM, and always helps for Duo. Since it is detrimental to MDLM at high NFE, we do not use nucleus sampling when using the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers, for both MDLM and Duo.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">How to Pick <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>?</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recommend a cosine sampling schedule with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{on}}\in\{0.5,0.6\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{on}}\in\{0.5,0.6\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{off}}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{off}}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}=0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}=0.99</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{on}}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{on}}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{off}}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{off}}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> for MDLM. We suggest using a piecewise constant <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with linearly decreasing <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than the ReMDM loop schedule (Suppl. <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 ReMDM Sampling Schedules ‣ Appendix A Ψ-Posteriors ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>), setting <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[t_{\text{off}},t_{\text{on}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[t_{\text{off}},t_{\text{on}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, since it outperforms the ReMDM schedules.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Fast Curriculum</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="89.91" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 89.91" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,89.91) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000B3;" fill="#0000B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 84.01 C 0 87.27 2.64 89.91 5.91 89.91 L 544.09 89.91 C 547.36 89.91 550 87.27 550 84.01 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E0E0FF;" fill="#E0E0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 84.01 C 1.97 86.18 3.73 87.94 5.91 87.94 L 544.09 87.94 C 546.27 87.94 548.03 86.18 548.03 84.01 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.75 8.67)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.2em;--ltx-fo-height:5.14em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="71.18" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 71.18)" width="528.58"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.2em;">
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway 5:<span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> </span>The efficient curriculum reduces peak memory by 33% and training time by 25%<span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, while matching the performance of Duo on likelihood benchmarks and downstream tasks.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental Settings</h5>

<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:39%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Test perplexity (PPL) on LM1B and OWT. Lower is better. <sup id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>Results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025a)</a></cite>. Best Uniform-state diffusion numbers are <span id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bolded</span>. Duo and <math id="S5.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> achieve comparable performance across both datasets while requiring 25% fewer GPU-hours (Table <a href="#A4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ D.3 Training Efficiency of Our Fast Curriculum ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental results ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), demonstrating the effectiveness of our memory-efficient curriculum.</figcaption>
