<td id="A4.T4.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A4.T4.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EBEBEB;">63.4</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ D.3 Training Efficiency of Our Fast Curriculum ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental results ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, our sparse curriculum achieves a 33% reduction in peak memory usage and reaches an average throughput 25% higher than Duo, at a context length of 1024.</p>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Zero-shot perplexity (PPL) on seven datasets. Lower is better. <sup id="A4.T5.3" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>Results taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2025a)</a></cite>. <math id="A4.T5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Duo}^{++}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>Duo</mtext><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Duo}^{++}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A4.T5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>) achieves a slightly lower zero-shot perplexity than Duo on 6 of 7 datasets.</figcaption>
