<figure id="A4.T11" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span>FID on CIFAR-10 using <math id="A4.T11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers whose <math id="A4.T11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> schedulers are equivalent to ReMDM. We use no nucleus sampling, no temperature scaling, and <math id="A4.T11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cfg}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cfg</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cfg}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. As expected, with the log-linear scheduler, we reach a similar FID as when using the ReMDM codebase (Table <a href="#A4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ D.3 Training Efficiency of Our Fast Curriculum ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental results ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). However, note that by using a log-linear scheduler, using a constant <math id="A4.T11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}=0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}=0.99</annotation></semantics></math>, we reach a better FID than with the original ReMDM scheduler.</figcaption>
