<figure id="A4.T14" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 14: </span>
Generative Perplexity (Gen. PPL) and Unigram Entropy on OpenWebText <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gokaslan and Cohen, 2019</a>)</cite> with <math id="A4.T14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>-samplers using <math id="A4.T14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> schedules matching ReMDM (log-linear step size) and <span id="A4.T14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">distilled models</span> (as in Table <a href="#A4.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ D.3 Training Efficiency of Our Fast Curriculum ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental results ‣ The Diffusion Duality, Chapter II: Ψ-Samplers and Efficient Curriculum" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). We experiment with nucleus sampling, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</figcaption>
